## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

To evaluate ability to design minimal-witness solvers for Horn-like rules and detect derivability with backtracking.

## Key Concepts

- Horn clauses
- Minimal witness extraction
- Forward-chaining with backtracking
- Cycle detection in derivations

## Code Example

```javascript
// simple forward-chaining for (A∧B)→C rules
function canDerive(target, base, rules){
  const derived = new Set(base);
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [A,B,C] of rules){
      if (derived.has(A) && derived.has(B) && !derived.has(C)){
        derived.add(C);
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return derived.has(target);
}
```

